{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/23385-designing-an-interactive-transgaming-crafting-experience","id":23385,"title":"Designing an interactive \"transgaming\" Crafting experience","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-12-04T16:49:44-08:00","id":23385,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Designing an interactive \"transgaming\" Crafting experience","content":"<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FoYSwGNGksQ\" target=\"_blank\">First, some mood music.</a><br /> <br /> <br /> I started a <a href=\"http://forums.whitewolfarchive.com/default67d4.html?g=posts&amp;t=79091\" target=\"_blank\">topic like this </a>one in the last forum, but this thread will have an entirely different focus. Since I was looking for a game which apparently does not exist, I would now rather discuss how one might <i>design </i>such a game.<br /> <br /> To explain:<br /> <br /> A <a href=\"http://&#39;http//www.penny-arcade.com/patv/episode/transgaming&#39;\" target=\"_blank\">transgaming</a> experience, as defined by the wonderful folks at Extra Credits, is where two completely different games are interconnected, such that the actions of one game may affect the other.<br /> <br /> What I would like to see, for example, is Blizzard making a <i>Warcraft </i>game (&quot;<i>Swordcraft</i>&quot;, maybe) which was about nothing but making swords (or other gear), where the player could put the items he creates up for sale on the Auction House of Blizzard's <i>World of Warcraft </i>MMO for purchase by the players of that other game.<br /> <br /> <i>WoW </i>players could also mail back custom orders and materials to my <i>Swordcraft</i> &quot;character's&quot; address, which I'd find when I logged in, and then I'd make the requested item and mail it back. Or I'd put an inventory up for sale, and players could visit a store and buy it, or engage in haggling-by-mail for a lower cost.<br /> <br /> There are already games which are focused entirely around the action of creating some product rather than engaging in battle. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6lh6bgz24ss\" target=\"_blank\"><i>Cooking Mama</i>, for example (Youtube link).</a> However, the food produced in <i>Cooking Mama </i>does not benefit anyone else. It cannot be eaten or enjoyed by another human being. Your accomplishment is almost completely private (except for other fans of the game who might be impressed if you succeed at a notable challenge) and is certainly, entirely transient.<br /> <br /> However, if <i>Cooking Mama </i>were connected to <i>World of Warcraft</i>, such that you could sell the food you make as consumable items, suddenly a much wider audience of human beings cares about your efforts and results. The same could be done with swords and magic rings.<br /> <br /> Further, the experience of the game could extent beyond the creation of the product to include the activity of marketing the product and selling to other players at a negotiated (or tyrannically inflated) price, without having to sacrifice focus on the crafting aspect.<br /> <br /> Blizzard could thus take their pre-existing and popular intellectual property of <i>Warcraft </i>and use it to reach a wider audience than even <i>WoW </i>currently attracts.<br /> <br /> <br /> Currently, crafting in <i>World of Warcraft </i>is a only a mini-game; a side-attraction next to the major components of &quot;combat&quot; and &quot;exploration&quot;. Necessarily, it recieved less focus and attention, which means less complexity and depth. It's meant more as a time-waster than as an engaging experience, requiring you to stamp out copy after copy of the same boring Plain Copper Sword to grind your skill so that you might make the Plain Steel Sword. Which you then have to tediously mass produce to make the next step up.<br /> <br /> Worse, if what I'm told is true, the gear you spend so much time making is not appreciably better than what you can buy from NPC vendors, and <i>less </i>desirable than the gear that one commonly finds as loot while exploring and fighting (which are, again, the two major activities around which the game is built).<br /> <br /> But suppose that Blizzard could offer you <i>two </i>games to play with the same character? Your dwarven blacksmith, rather than playing an afterthought mini-game within <i>WoW</i>, could instead apply his resources and relevant stats to a completely different game which is entirely designed around making things <i>as an enjoyable experience</i>.<br /> <br /> It's win-win, really. Blizzard still gets your money, and you actually derive more enjoyment from the time and money you spend.<br /> <br /> Or, if you don't already have a dwarven adventurer and don't care to start one, the dwarf you create can happily remain a Level 1 character of indeterminate Class (as he never leaves -- or even technically appears within -- the starting zone), happily spending all of his time smithing at a forge and shipping off his finished goods. If or when you ever decide that you DO want to experience the World of Warcraft as an adventurer, you can take the character you've already created, pick a Class, and get going!<br /> <br /> So Blizzard wins even more, by potentially luring in more players who would otherwise have not given their MMO a chance.<br /> <br /> <br /> But... how would one design this crafting game? Keeping in mind that just as World of Warcraft is built around two very different experiences (exploration and combat), this game would also be built around two very different (but strangely parallel!) experiences: &quot;Design&quot; and &quot;Production&quot;.<br /> <br /> &quot;Production&quot; is the portion of the game that focuses on the challenge of &quot;how good can I make this item?&quot; The <i>Cooking Mama </i>game is an excellent example. One develops skill at the execution of the action. This is analogous to combat, requiring reflexes and quick decision-making under a time limit, with strict pass/fail conditions, aided by memorization of the patterns and rhythms of particular tasks (as you would do with particular enemies in combat).<br /> <br /> &quot;Design&quot; is coming up your own &quot;recipes&quot;. In the example of forging a magical sword, it's experimenting with different combinations of carved runes in order to make better swords. This aspect of the game (analogous to exploration), is probably the one which will require the most discussion, for the purpose of making it both challenging and fun.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":636,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","user":"Sunder the Gold","date":". 12-04-2013, 07:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-04T22:55:37-08:00","id":23551,"title":"","content":"minecraft. Eve online. WoD:MMO. All games with a strong craft focus. Hell, even Ultima Online.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-05T00:20:00-08:00","id":23587,"title":"","content":"It's a cool idea. I was hoping Blizzard's Hearthstone game (the online CCG) would tie into Warcraft somehow, even if it was just a peripheral way of getting things like mounts, pets or appearance-only gear. <br /> <br /> More on topic, when designing such a game, especially one involving crafting, within a day of release all of the &quot;tricks&quot; to getting the &quot;best&quot; recipes will be up online and everyone will follow it to make the &quot;best&quot; stuff.. And if it ties into WoW and has rewards, then it's safe to assume that everyone is only going to be making the best recipes. So you might want to introduce an element of chance. Say, when someone gets an &quot;Excellent&quot; or &quot;Perfect&quot; rating while crafting, it adds a gemslot or something similar. <br /> <br /> The easiest method would be a general mini-game (a popular one right now is Candy Crush). Where, if you have the materials you can play one of a dozen mini-games, and then, as a reward, you get a weapon with various bonuses depending on how well you did. <br /> <br /> Interesting thread though, I'll be paying attention to it.<br /> <br /> AnubisXy","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-05T11:36:39-08:00","id":23856,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/23385-designing-an-interactive-transgaming-crafting-experience?p=23587#post23587\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">More on topic, when designing such a game, especially one involving crafting, within a day of release all of the &quot;tricks&quot; to getting the &quot;best&quot; recipes will be up online and everyone will follow it to make the &quot;best&quot; stuff.. And if it ties into WoW and has rewards, then it's safe to assume that everyone is only going to be making the best recipes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like I said, the &quot;design&quot; aspect will probably require the most thought, for providing fun and a challenge.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So you might want to introduce an element of chance. Say, when someone gets an &quot;Excellent&quot; or &quot;Perfect&quot; rating while crafting, it adds a gemslot or something similar. </div> </div> </div>Absolutely not.<br /> <br /> Some random number generation may be included, but the Production aspect of the game should not be reduced to a One-Armed Bandit.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The easiest method would be a general mini-game (a popular one right now is Candy Crush). Where, if you have the materials you can play one of a dozen mini-games, and then, as a reward, you get a weapon with various bonuses depending on how well you did. </div> </div> </div>That is exactly what I was driving at, yes.<br /> <br /> In fact, a single finished product (a sturdy magical sword blessed by the gods) might require multiple and various minigames. Perhaps working a forge requires a different puzzle compared to the act of cutting magical gemstones or engraving runes of power or calling down the blessings of the gods.<br /> <br /> So a player bad at the forging mini-game but awesome at the blessing mini-game will produce mediocre swords with amazing divine blessings. Which distinguishes him as a craftsmen against the likes of those players produce excellent weapons with shoddy, unreliable enchantments.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-07T10:14:07-08:00","id":25212,"title":"","content":"If we're sticking with the Swordcraft idea here, then I think you need to leave recipe based crafting behind. If you know that you're going to get a weapon with a particular set of stats at the end of the crafting set up, there's going to be a rush on the &quot;best&quot; recipes and those will be easily determined, since you can easily compare two different items to see which one has the best stat bonuses.<br /> <br /> I think a better solution would be to create a set of materials, each of which provides a particular type of bonus to the item (like adding black iron to the item increases strength), limit how many types of material you can add to one item, define a bunch of item types (swords, shields, etc) which maybe have some base bonuses or benefits, and then have a bunch of crafting mini-games based on, say, bejeweled, those slider block puzzles, rhythm games, games like Trauma Center, or all of the above, and how well you execute each game determines how big the bonus for a given material is, whether your blessing worked, how many gem slots it has, etc.<br /> <br /> The connection points to WoW would be that materials cost money, but WoW players can give/sell you materials at lower prices than the NPC Material Bank, while you can in turn, can give/sell your created items at similarly economical prices.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-07T10:36:29-08:00","id":25227,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/23385-designing-an-interactive-transgaming-crafting-experience?p=23551#post23551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">minecraft. Eve online. WoD:MMO. All games with a strong craft focus. Hell, even Ultima Online.</div> </div> </div> </div>Minecraft has a very interesting crafting system. At first, it just looks like your standard &quot;spend lots of time getting materials and two seconds on the actual crafting&quot; set up, but I recently realized that pretty much all the items you make directly are either food, tools to let you get more materials*, or tools which let you manipulate your environment**.<br /> <br /> So, if you look at the game as a pure crafting experience (which certainly isn't the only way to look at it), the point of the game ultimately ends up being creating an infrastructure base which lets you create enough &quot;manipulate your environment tools&quot; to let you achieve whatever your actual end product goal is, like a Babbage Machine, Slot Machine, Pixel Art, Best Roller-coaster Ever, etc. (see youtube for examples). The actual crafting system is &quot;Here are the materials and tools to which you have access. What can you make with them?&quot;<br /> <br /> ...I doubt I'm the first person to have figured this out, but it seemed like an important point to make in this context.<br /> <br /> <br /> * Picks, shovels, shears, but also stuff like torches and swords which let you not die in mines and kill monsters for certain rare materials.<br /> ** Pretty much anything that uses redstone but water and lava might count here. Also standard material blocks. Anything that lets you build a big cool thing comes in here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-07T11:54:20-08:00","id":25272,"title":"","content":"Ultima Online was the SHIT. my friend did nothing but be a blacksmith. he ran his shop and was 100/100 in it. it was pretty cool.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>pls respont","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/372-cerealkiller","name":"cerealkiller","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar372_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"340"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-25T15:25:06-08:00","id":57681,"title":"","content":"EVE does something somewhat related, in the territory management area. Sort of. Dust 514 is a shooter that is connected to the main game and efforts on either side impact the other, or so I've heard.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1148-palemask","name":"Palemask","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-25T17:03:29-08:00","id":57751,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>cerealkiller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/23385-designing-an-interactive-transgaming-crafting-experience?p=25272#post25272\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ultima Online was the SHIT. my friend did nothing but be a blacksmith. he ran his shop and was 100/100 in it. it was pretty cool.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm actually quite curious as to how the new thing turns out since it's sort of an MMO and sort of not an MMO.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-26T09:30:56-08:00","id":58132,"title":"","content":"So, do you already know for what game you want to make a transgame? A crafting game that fits the setting of Exalted could end up quite different from one that fits the setting of Cyberpunk 2020","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>You need a picture altered to fit your Exalted character, or just looking for some visual inspiration? Check out the twice-reborn <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/266-photoshop-exaltification\" target=\"_blank\"> Exaltification thread</a>. And here is my <a href=\"http://danelsan.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Deviantart page</a><br /> <br /> S\u00f8ren Kierkegaard + Kim Kardashian = <a href=\"https://twitter.com/KimKierkegaard\" target=\"_blank\">Brilliance</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/868-danelsan","name":"danelsan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar868_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}